\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We studied how a streaming respiratory sound detector can decide which of its expensive state updates to skip. The model keeps a persistent graph of frequency regions, observes every chunk with a cheap encoder, and updates only a budget-limited set of regions chosen by a learned controller. The key change from AGN-Net is the training target of the controller: it predicts how much skipping a region's update would change the model's output, not how much the region's representation will change. With the same number of node updates, this controller preserves the full-update model's detections far better than random, periodic, energy-based and change-based selection, and better than a gate of the same size trained on the task loss on top of the frozen model, at every budget and in every seed. The advantage holds for two cells, and the advantage over change-based and random selection also holds for a harmonic graph topology and on a second, adult dataset, so it is a property of the selection method rather than of one backbone. With a quarter of the graph updates, the AGN-Net cell's event F against the annotations on SPRSound is non-inferior to full updating; in the other settings it is missed at 25\% on the 2024 set or on HF\_Lung (narrowly for the graph GRU on SPRSound and the AGN-Net cell on HF\_Lung, more clearly for the harmonic graph and for the graph GRU on HF\_Lung) and holds at 50\% in all settings. The analyses explain why: skip-harm utility is concentrated in a few regions and predictable from information available at inference time, the one-step target ranks regions well by their multi-step harm, and the state error that a selective update causes lies mostly in directions the readout ignores, which a state-level objective cannot see. The advantage is most plausibly due to the training target, although we tested only two cells, two graph topologies and two datasets.

The work has limitations. On SPRSound the test releases follow a different annotation style, so absolute event scores are low for every model and below those of offline detectors built for this benchmark; boundary-tolerant metrics show that much of the low strict-collar score comes from boundary conventions. The AGN-Net cell was not a better detector than a plain graph GRU; harmonic graph edges made it comparable in event F and much more stable, but it stayed below the graph GRU on the adventitious metrics, and on HF\_Lung the AGN-Net cell occasionally diverged during training. The end-to-end speedup at our model size is modest ({{rt_numpy_util@0.25_speedup|2f}}$\times$ at 25\% of the updates in a lightweight runtime, none in PyTorch eager mode), because the controller costs a large part of the time it saves; the overhead shrinks for larger cells, but we did not measure on embedded hardware. The skip-harm target is a one-step proxy whose ranking we tested for one stale region at a time, not for many regions stale together; the end-to-end gate was trained on a frozen backbone with untuned settings; and our longest streams last two minutes, so the refresh rule was tested only where staleness stays moderate. Proposition~\ref{prop:bound} is conditional, and its link to the output is loose.

Future work includes cheaper or amortised controllers, learned and adaptive graph topologies, block-structured selection for embedded accelerators where sparse updates translate into energy savings, and evaluation on long-term wearable recordings. All code, logs, per-recording results and trained weights are public at {{repo_link}}.
